lesson:
Three loops:

```
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {      // counting: start; keep going while; step
    System.out.println(i);
}

int total = 0;
while (total < 100) {                // until something happens
    total += 30;
}

for (String crop : crops) {          // every item in an array or list
    System.out.println(crop);
}
```

`i++` adds 1 (`i--` subtracts, `i += 5` steps by 5). The variable `i` made in the for exists only inside the loop.

As everywhere, something must make a `while` condition false, or it never ends. `break` leaves the loop; `continue` jumps to the next round.

Patterns you'll use forever: **accumulate** (start `total = 0` before, add inside, use after), **count** (`count++` when something matches), **find the biggest** (start with the first item, replace when you see bigger), and **nested loops** for grids and tables (the inner loop runs fully for each round of the outer).

example.code:
public class Example {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
                System.out.print(i * j + "\t");
            }
            System.out.println();
        }
        int saved = 0, months = 0;
        while (saved < 1000) {
            saved += 300;
            months++;
        }
        System.out.println(months + " months");
        for (String c : new String[] {"maize", "beans"}) System.out.println(c);
    }
}

